Decision
================================================================================

The repository root carries an Apache-2.0 LICENSE, but sections 1 and 4 above
are GPL-2.0-or-later. GPL-2.0-only code cannot be combined with Apache-2.0.
GPL-2.0-**or-later** code may be distributed under GPL-3.0 instead, and
Apache-2.0 -> GPL-3.0 is a permitted combination (GPLv3 section 10), so the tree
can be made consistent. Three options were considered:

  A. Keep HyperDex's own code Apache-2.0 and state that the combined work, and
     any file importing contracts/vendor/v3-core, is distributed under
     GPL-3.0-or-later.

  B. Relicense the whole repository GPL-3.0-or-later, which is what Uniswap's
     own v3-periphery does. Simplest to reason about; requires every copyright
     holder to agree. This repository's history has more than one author
     identity, so consent is not automatic.

  C. Stay purely Apache-2.0 by not shipping v3 code at all: interact with
     deployed Uniswap v3 pools through interfaces only, and drop the vendored
     tree. This changes the deployment model, since HyperDex currently deploys
     its own pools.

**Option A has been chosen.** Not yet applied to the tree, so until the steps
below land the repository must still not be described as plain Apache-2.0:

  1. Add the GPL-3.0 text as LICENSE-GPL-3.0 alongside the existing LICENSE.
  2. State in LICENSE that Apache-2.0 covers HyperDex's own code only, and that
     the combined work is GPL-3.0-or-later.
  3. Change the SPDX header of every file that imports contracts/vendor/v3-core
     to `MIT OR GPL-3.0-or-later`. At the time of writing that is
     contracts/HyperDex.sol and contracts/HyperDexFactory.sol.
  4. Update the licensing section of README.md to match.

Option A does not require consent from other copyright holders, because it does
not relicense anyone's existing code - it only states the terms under which the
combined derivative is distributed.
